  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-2x$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,2)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,\\infty )$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-2 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x=12x(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x(x-2) & >0 \\\\ x(x-2) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/cb06747b13b36bc1b53f96df8d1345332200ecb8b7728342d7086963b51d86f5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-2)>0$ when $x<0$ and $x>2.$ So the graph is concave up on the intervals  $(-\\infty ,0)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-2)<0,$ which has the solution $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
